      "text": "Let's first write out the given numbers to simplify the addition:\n\n$$\\hspace{25 mm} 3^0, 3^0 + 3^1, 3^0 + 3^1 + 3^2$$\n\nWhen we add up these numbers in the problem, the second number will have a factor of 3 to the $0$ exponent, the third number will have a factor of 3 to the $1$ exponent, and the fourth number will have a factor of 3 to the $2$ exponent:\n\n\\begin{align\\*}\n&3^0 + 3^0 + 3^1  + 3^2 \\\\\\text{Simplified as:}\\\n&3^{0} (1 +1 + 3 + 9)\\\\\\text{Simplified as:}\\\n&3^{0} (5 + 3^{1} (1+ 1))\\\\\\text{Simplified as:}\\\n&3^{0} (5 + 3^{1} (2))\\\\\\text{Simplified as:}\\\n&3^{0} (5 + 2 \\cdot 3^{1} )\\\\\\text{Simplified as:}\\\n&3^{0} (5 + 6)\n\\end{align\\*}\n\nYes, the 3's are correct. Continuing the calculation to get the simplified total will give us:\n\n$$\\hspace{25 mm} 3^0 + 3^0 + 3^1  + 3^2 = 3^{0} (5+ 6)= 3^{0} (11)= 3 \\cdot 11$$\n\nTherefore, as Skyman wrote in his calculation, 3 times 11 is 33 so the fourth term in the sequence is 33. The answer is 33.",
