      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "We are asked to find the value of $3^0 + 3^1+ 3^2 + 3^3$.\nBy adding consecutive powers of 3, the sum can be simplified as a geometric series.\n\nThe first term $a$ of the series is $3^0=1$, and the common ratio $r$ is also $3^0=1$.\n\nThe sum of the first $n$ terms of a geometric series is given by the formula: $$S =\\dfrac{a(1-r^n)}{1-r}$$\n\nPlugging in the values, we have:\n\n$$S_{n} =\\dfrac{1(1-3^n)}{1-3}= \\dfrac{1-3^n}{-2}$$\n\nSubstituting $n=4$, we have:\n\n$$S_{4} = \\dfrac{1-3^4}{-2} = \\dfrac{-30}{-2} = \\boxed{15}$$\n\nThe sum of the first 4 terms of the sequence is 15.",
